2. Function Definitions

In this section you will write all of the function definitions that you will use in this lab adventure to validate the id. Make your function names, the parameters and returns match the function specification exactly.

RECALL from Lab 1: That a valid ID is {sectornum}{4 digit tempid}{sectornum}{reverse tempid}{checksum}


2A. is_number function
This function will determine if a string is all numbers or not

    Parameter: id as a string
    Return: True if string is all digits, False if not


2B. is_correct_len function
This function will determine if a string's length is the same as a parameter. Will return whether the length is correct, too long or too short.

    Parameter: id as a string, correct length
    Return: a string - "correct", "short", "long"



2C. is_valid_sector function
This function will determine if the sector number appears twice in the id in the correct location.

    Parameter: id as a string
    Return: True if valid, False if not



2D. is_valid_tempid function
This function will determine if the 4 digit tempid in the id number is equal to the reversed four digits.

    Parameters: id as a string
    Return: True if valid, False if not



2E. is_valid_checksum function
This function will determine if final character of the id is a valid checksum. Recall that the checksum is the sum of the first 10 digits mod 10.

    Parameters: id as a string
    Return:
        If valid: True, calculated checksum
        If invalid: False, calculated checksum



2F. is_valid_id function
This function uses the helper functions above to determine if an id is valid.

    Parameters: id as a string
    Return:
        It will return whether it is valid or not. If it is valid it returns the valid checksum.
        If not, it will return the reason why not.
            Reasons are limited to:
                "non-numeric"
                "too short"
                "too long"
                "invalid tempid"
                "invalid sectors"
                "invalid checksum"



3. Main Code

You will be processing a captured signal to analyze the employee IDs inside. You are given a test set to make sure your functions are working correctly (TEST_SIGNALS). You know which ones are valid, invalid, spoofed or corrupt. You can use these individually or as a whole list.

You are also given INTERCEPTED_SIGNALS that you need to process correctly. You do not know which ones are valid, invalid, spoofed or corrupt, so will have to trust your functions. In this set there will be:

    7 valid ids
    4 invalid ids (too long or short)
    5 spoofed ids (bad temp id, sectors, or checksum)
    3 corrupt ids (non-numeric)


